{"i": 0, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"lost‑wax sprue tree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Lost-Wax Sprue Tree\n\n'Twas brillig, and the waxen blobs\nDid gyre and gimble in the sand;\nAll gooey were the moulden throbs,\nAnd the sprue-tree out of hand.\n\n\"Beware the Sprue-Tree, oh my son!\nThe branches rough, the pour-holes deep!\nBeware the draggle-cast, and shun\nThe Snarling Shrinkage-Creep!\"\n\nHe took his melta-blade in hand:\nLong time the faulty foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Flask-block stand,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in polished thought he stood,\nThe Sprue-Tree, with its sprut-sprut sound,\nCame whiffling through the grimy wood,\nAnd burbled as it wound!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe melta-blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its crew\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Sprue-Tree foe?\nBring forth the ingot, oh my beam!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his dream.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the waxen blobs\nDid gyre and gimble in the sand;\nAll gooey were the moulden throbs,\nAnd the sprue-tree out of hand.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 1, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"kintsugi seam of gold\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Kintsugi Seam of Gold**\n\n‘Twas glimbering, and the sherd-spikes grabbled\nIn the wobbly-wood where the mended-ware gleamed;\nAll widdershins were the cracks that dabbled,\nAnd the bluster-bugs beautifully beamed.\n\n“Beware the Grumblecracks, my son!\nThe jaws that gnaw, the claws that patch!\nBeware the Fissuregums, and shun\nThe frumious splinter-snatch!”\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand;\nLong time the mimsy foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Grumblecrack, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n“And hast thou slain the Grumblecrack?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!”\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n‘Twas glimbering, and the sherd-spikes grabbled\nIn the wobbly-wood where the mended-ware gleamed;\nAll widdershins were the cracks that dabbled,\nAnd the bluster-bugs beautifully beamed.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 2, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"alpenglow on scree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "The Glimmering of the Cragglehorn\n\n'Twas roseate, and the cragglehorns\n      Did shimmer with an alpin glow;\nAll mimsy were the scree-borne stones,\n      And the bouldergraves did wainly go.\n\n\"Beware the Scree-Snarl, oh my son!\n      The jagged tooth, the tumbling claw!\nBeware the Alp-Hag, and shun\n      The frumious Rock-Shriek's maw!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal axe in hand;\n      Long time the fructive foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumbling-strand,\n      And stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\n      The Scree-Snarl, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the alpen wood,\n      And burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\n      The vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its hue\n      He went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Scree-Snarl?\n      Come to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\n      He chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas roseate, and the cragglehorns\n      Did shimmer with an alpin glow;\nAll mimsy were the scree-borne stones,\n      And the bouldergraves did wainly go.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 3, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"plane iron camber\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Planecronk's Glimble\n\n'Twas brillig, and the planecronk did soar,\nThe cambering giblets in the air did gleam;\nAll gimbly were the wing-flaps, and the bore\nOf propellor-whizzle made a screeble-scream.\n\n\"Beware the Iron-Scrotch, my son! The teeth\nThat gnab, the claws that catch, the engine's fume!\nBeware the Camber-Blast, and shun the heath\nWhere dwell the Grumble-Grumblers in the gloom!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal joystick in his hand:\nLong time the Planecronk foe he sought.\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree, and planned\nHis glumble-flight, in deep and dizzy thought.\n\nAnd, as in biffish thought he stood,\nThe Iron-Scrotch, with eyes of fiery red,\nCame whiffling through the turbid wood,\nAnd burbled as it flappled overhead!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal joystick went snick-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Iron-Scrotch?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the planecronk did soar,\nThe cambering giblets in the air did gleam;\nAll gimbly were the wing-flaps, and the bore\nOf propellor-whizzle made a screeble-scream.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 4, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"badger knot bloom\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Badgerknotted Bloom\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toads\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe:\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.\n\n\"Beware the Badgerknotted Bloom, my son!\nThe jaws that snap, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Jujub bird, and shun\nThe frumious Bandersnatch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand;\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Badgerknotted Bloom, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Badgerknotted Bloom?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toads\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe:\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 5, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"summit prayer flag\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Summit Prayer-Flag**\n\n'Twas high-peak, and the windlestream did whifflest through the cloud;\nAll flitterflaps were prayer-plapped, and mumble-muttered loud.\nThe yeti-foot was crunching-slow, the ice-grim did descend;\nBeware the Summit Prayer-Flag, my son! The woe-woe without end!\n\nHe took his ice-axe in his hand: Long time the snowy woe he sought—\nSo rested he by the rock-clutch tree, and stood awhile in thought.\nAnd, as in frostful thought he stood, the Summit Prayer-Flag, with eyes aflame,\nCame whizzing through the blizz-blaze snow, and whippled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through the ice-axe went snicker-snick!\nHe left it dead, and with its head he went galumphing quick.\n“And hast thou slain the Summit Prayer-Flag? Come to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!” He chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas high-peak, and the windlestream did whifflest through the cloud;\nAll flitterflaps were prayer-plapped, and mumble-muttered loud.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 6, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"slipjoint walk and talk\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Slipjoint Strutter**\n\n'Twas a slipjoint walk, and the talk did sprool,\nAll mimberish the mimsy wight;\nThe whiffling grins, with a babble a-drool,\nDid bletherspark the night.\n\n\"Beware the Slipjoint, my son, my son!\nThe clappergab, the flibberty-flee!\nBeware the chatterchumps, and shun\nThe jabberly jabber-tee!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal pen in hand;\nLong time the frumious foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum stand,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Slipjoint, with its eyes aflame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne two! One two! And through and through\nThe vorpal pen went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its crew\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Slipjoint blight?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas a slipjoint walk, and the talk did sprool,\nAll mimberish the mimsy wight;\nThe whiffling grins, with a babble a-drool,\nDid bletherspark the night.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 7, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"rain on a tin roof\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Tin-Roofed Pitter-Patter**\n\n'Twas splishy, and the drippy drops\nDid gyre and gibble on the tin;\nAll mimsy were the attic-tops,\nAnd the downspout did begin.\n\nBeware the Squishes, oh my soul!\nThe Plonky-Plonks, with clatter and crash!\nBeware the Dribbles, and the whole\nWetness that made a watery dash!\n\nHe took his ear, and listened long;\nThen, with his thoughts in a puddle of drear,\nHe sought the sound, so plinky-song,\nAnd found it near, and oh so clear.\n\nAnd as in thought he stood, the Drops,\nWith eyes a-gleam and slicky sheen,\nCame thumping through the gutter-stops,\nA-squishing through the roofy scene!\n\nOne-two! One-two! And through and through\nThe tin-roof grobbled, flap and thrum!\nHe left it dripping, wet and new,\nAnd flew back whence he had become.\n\n\"And hast thou heard the Squishes fall?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nOh, drippy day! Hooray! Hooray!\"\nHe chortled in his rainy joy.\n\n'Twas splishy, and the drippy drops\nDid gyre and gibble on the tin;\nAll mimsy were the attic-tops,\nAnd the downspout did begin.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 8, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"makeready under tympan\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Typanocky\n\n'Twas brillig, and the makeready, too,\nDid gyre and gimble in the press's maw:\nAll mimsy were the tympan's hue,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabed with awe.\n\n\"Beware the Makeready, my son!\nThe jaws that bite, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Gripper-snatch, and shun\nThe frumious Tympan-atch!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal knife in hand:\nLong time the man-print foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum Stand,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Makeready, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its crew\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Makeready?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the makeready, too,\nDid gyre and gimble in the press's maw:\nAll mimsy were the tympan's hue,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabed with awe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 9, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"modular synth patch spaghetti\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Modulon's Twine**\n\n'Twas patchig, and the spag-hetti did twine\nAnd gnarble through the rack all bright;\nAll whimsering were the cables, and the line\nOf signals blipped with blinking light.\n\nBeware the Modulon, my son!\nThe current that shorts, the hum and the whine!\nBeware the Fuzzbox, and shun\nThe frumious Oscillator's twine!\n\nHe took his patcher-sword in hand;\nLong time the Analog foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Filter's stand,\nAnd stood a while in silent thought.\n\nAnd, as in dreamy thought he stood,\nThe Modulon, with eyes of flame,\nCame sizzling through the wooded wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe patcher-blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its screw\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Modulon?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas patchig, and the spag-hetti did twine\nAnd gnarble through the rack all bright;\nAll whimsering were the cables, and the line\nOf signals blipped with blinking light.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
